==Evolution==
Evolves into '''Frog Ninja Cat''' at level 10.

Evolves into '''Flying Ninja Cat''' when obtained from [[Ninja Awakens!]] (may drop on the first stage but guaranteed on the second) and is at level 20 or above.

==Strategy/Usage==
Ninja Cat is extremely potent early on, having straight-up better stats than [[Axe Cat (Normal Cat)|Axe Cat]]. It is recommended to buy her as early as possible, as she alone can carry through the whole first, second and the early portion of the third chapter, but her use rapidly declines afterwards, quickly becoming outclassed by Dark Cat (whose + levels give him an edge) and [[Crazed Axe Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Crazed Axe Cat]], two units that are not very useful to begin with. And that is to say nothing about the more efficient anti-red specialists, such as [[Ancient Egg: N101 (Rare Cat)|Courier]] and [[Swordsman Cat (Rare Cat)|Elemental Duelist]], or even [[Viking Cat (Rare Cat)|Maximum the Fighter]].

Though she becomes obsolete for actual use, Ninja Cat is also part of the [[Cool Japan (Combo)|Cool Japan]] [[Cat Combo]], which provides a 15% attack boost to all your units; this is a very useful Combo and is worth bringing into many stages. Unfortunately, even this becomes mostly obsolete once 2 Sm Attack combos are obtained, as they provide a 20% boost to attack instead of just 15% and their units are only a little less accessible ([[Tiny Ancient Hunt (Combo)|Tiny Ancient Hunt]] and [[Brave Cutter (Combo)|Brave Cutter]]). This isn't helped by the release of [[Mighty Deth-Troy-R (Uber Rare Cat)|Mighty Deth-Troy-R]] which provides the same boost for half the slots for players lucky enough to own it.

==='''True Form'''===
Flying Ninja Cat becomes slower and cheaper. Her stats are still not usable, and while the speed penalty allows you to create a thicker line of defense, her cost is way too high. Thus, regardless of the minor improvements, Flying Ninja Cat is still of no practical use without [[Talents]].

==='''Talents'''===
Flying Ninja Cat's usage as a meatshield is improved dramatically by her Dodge Attack talent as, with it, Flying Ninja Cat goes from being little more than a more expensive [[Cat (Normal Cat)|Mohawk Cat]] in practice to a unit who can hold off decently well against even the strongest Red enemies, although it can be rather inconsistent than other options to defeat them outright. Move Speed Up helps her out a lot too, as it allows meatshields to be generally more effective and to clog the unit limit less.

Since Flying Ninja Cat's base stats are really low, HP Buff and Attack Buff do very little, even with her Strong ability factored in. Target [[:Category:Dark Enemies|Dark]] is only useful with Dodge Attack to deny them from pushing briefly. Since Dark enemies tend to deal extreme overall damage and can push just as aggressively as Reds, it's safe to assume that Flying Ninja Cat will die immediately if she doesn't dodge, and even then, she can be just as inconsistent as against Reds due to the wide variety of other Anti-Dark units. Overall, optionally worth the investments, but the NP could be better spent elsewhere.

==='''Orb'''===
Flying Ninja Cat relies on health rather than damage in later game, so give her Defense Up vs Red or Defense Up vs Dark. However, those two traits have extreme pushing power ([[Capy]], [[Shadow Boxer K]], [[Professor A.]], [[Bun Bun Black]], etc), she's not worth the orb and only used as a Dodge meatshield if talented. While a Dodge Orb might appear to be a good option, the way Dodge is calculated in the game makes the bonus inconsequential against targets (raising it to a measly 28% at most), making it nigh-useless outside of mixed stages.

{{Job|Classification = Meatshield & Combo Slave}}

